; island.mx -- a rewrite over real C, in text mode. Stage 5's rules.
;
; The body this is used on is not written to fit a grammar: it is
; metaxis/cmd/mx.c, the tool's own front end, exactly as it is in the tree.
; Text mode is an island grammar by construction -- a rule fires where it
; matches and everything else is copied through -- so a file that declares
; two constructs can be pointed at a real file and leave the rest alone.
; That is what the survey in docs/prior-art.md §3.3 said was the strongest
; thing this tool lacked, and text mode turned out to have it already.
;
; The rewrite: every `fprintf(stderr, "mx: %s\n", X)` becomes `complain(X)`,
; and `complain` is defined in front of `usage`, which is the one line that
; file has where a definition can go. That landmark is a workaround and says
; so: the honest place is *before the first function*, and nothing here can
; say that -- docs/ROADMAP.md 4 is the same wall from the BASIC side.
;
; What text mode does not know, measured on this file and recorded in
; docs/ROADMAP.md 7: it has no tokens, so a word fires inside an identifier or
; a string; it has no brackets, so a hole over `f(x, g(y))` stops at the first
; `)` and is right only when the template keeps the hole last; and declaring
; C's comments would remove them. The two rules below are written to stay
; inside what it does know.
;
; Two things about writing C from a string template. `{` is a hole, so C's
; braces are `{{ }}`; and `\n` is a newline, so the C source's own `\n` is
; `\\n`. A code template has neither problem and is the form to reach for
; when the C gets longer than a line.

@mode text

@syntax "fprintf(stderr, \"mx: %s\\n\", " e ")"  => "complain({e})"

@syntax "static void usage(void)"
    => "static void complain(const char *e) {{ fprintf(stderr, \"mx: %s\\n\", e); }}\n\nstatic void usage(void)"
